<h2 class="font-headline-md text-headline-md text-primary mb-4">Types of Facial Cellulitis</h2>
</div>
<div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-8">
<div class="bg-surface-container-lowest p-8 rounded-xl shadow-sm border border-surface-variant"><span class="material-symbols-outlined text-4xl text-secondary mb-4 icon-fill-1">healing</span><h3 class="font-headline-sm text-xl text-primary mb-3">Preseptal (Periorbital) Cellulitis</h3><p class="text-on-surface-variant">Infection of the eyelid and tissues in front of the orbital septum. Causes eyelid swelling, redness, and pain. Usually from a nearby skin infection, sinus infection, or hordeolum. Treated with oral or IV antibiotics depending on severity.</p></div>
<div class="bg-surface-container-lowest p-8 rounded-xl shadow-sm border border-surface-variant"><span class="material-symbols-outlined text-4xl text-secondary mb-4 icon-fill-1">healing</span><h3 class="font-headline-sm text-xl text-primary mb-3">Orbital Cellulitis</h3><p class="text-on-surface-variant">A serious infection behind the orbital septum involving the fat and muscles within the eye socket. Causes eye protrusion, painful eye movement, double vision, and vision changes. Requires urgent IV antibiotics and possible surgical drainage.</p></div>
<div class="bg-surface-container-lowest p-8 rounded-xl shadow-sm border border-surface-variant"><span class="material-symbols-outlined text-4xl text-secondary mb-4 icon-fill-1">healing</span><h3 class="font-headline-sm text-xl text-primary mb-3">Buccal Cellulitis</h3><p class="text-on-surface-variant">Infection of the cheek tissues — often from a dental source. Causes cheek swelling, redness, and warmth. Can spread to the buccal space and require surgical drainage if not treated early.</p></div>
<div class="bg-surface-container-lowest p-8 rounded-xl shadow-sm border border-surface-variant"><span class="material-symbols-outlined text-4xl text-secondary mb-4 icon-fill-1">healing</span><h3 class="font-headline-sm text-xl text-primary mb-3">Dental Origin Cellulitis</h3><p class="text-on-surface-variant">Cheek or facial cellulitis originating from a tooth infection. The infection spreads from the tooth apex into facial soft tissue. Requires both antibiotics (for the cellulitis) and dental source treatment (root canal or extraction).</p></div>
<div class="bg-surface-container-lowest p-8 rounded-xl shadow-sm border border-surface-variant"><span class="material-symbols-outlined text-4xl text-secondary mb-4 icon-fill-1">healing</span><h3 class="font-headline-sm text-xl text-primary mb-3">Erysipelas of the Face</h3><p class="text-on-surface-variant">A streptococcal infection of the upper dermal layers creating a sharply raised, bright red border. Typically affects the cheeks and bridge of the nose with a characteristic "butterfly" distribution. Responds well to penicillin antibiotics.</p></div>
<div class="bg-surface-container-lowest p-8 rounded-xl shadow-sm border border-surface-variant"><span class="material-symbols-outlined text-4xl text-secondary mb-4 icon-fill-1">healing</span><h3 class="font-headline-sm text-xl text-primary mb-3">Post-Traumatic Cellulitis</h3><p class="text-on-surface-variant">Skin infection developing after a facial laceration, abrasion, or surgical wound. Treated with wound care, antibiotics, and close monitoring for abscess formation. See our <a href="face-redness-and-swelling-treatment-hyderabad.php" class="text-secondary underline hover:text-primary">face redness and swelling</a> page for treatment details.</p></div>
